\maketitle
\section{Introduction}
Northeastern Thai, or Isan (ISO 639-3 tts; Glottocode nort2741), is spoken by roughly 15 million people in Thailand. Thai authorities treat it as a Thai dialect, while many linguists classify it as a Lao variety. It is written today in the Thai script, and the older Tai Noi script is no longer in everyday use. This search found little beyond overviews and scripture listings.
\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No Isan textbooks or school programmes were found in search.
\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Wikipedia, Isan language: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isan_language}
\item Omniglot, Isan writing: \url{https://www.omniglot.com/writing/isan.htm}
\item The Isaan Record, on the Preecha Phinthong dictionary (mentions other print and online dictionaries, with no titles given): \url{https://theisaanrecord.co/2017/08/02/tale-two-dictionaries-elegy-preecha-phinthong/?print=pdf}
\end{itemize}
\section{Religious and Community Resources}
Joshua Project lists Bible portions (1972--1996) and a New Testament (2016), with no complete Bible reported.
\begin{itemize}
\item Joshua Project, language profile for tts: \url{https://www.joshuaproject.net/index.php/languages/tts}
\item Joshua Project, Isan Thai in Thailand: \url{https://joshuaproject.net/people_groups/15460/th}
\end{itemize}
\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
Joshua Project pages point to Isan audio scripture and Global Recordings Network audio. I did not retrieve direct links to those recordings.
\section{Curriculum Development Context}
Isan is not known to be taught in schools. The Isaan Record notes that there is no written standard, so spelling conventions would need to be agreed before textbooks can be written.
